{"text": "Initially. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> The catchphrase less is more. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Had a simple meaning. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> First mentioned in Robert Brownings poem Andrea del Sarto. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> It suggests. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> That everything simple is better and more beautiful. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Than the complex and tangled. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Nowadays this phrase is heard often. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> , maybe even too often. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> But it is important to recognize. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> That the way of thinking that lies behind these words. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Slowly extinguishes certain habits. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> From our daily life. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> For example, think about. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> The heavy, massive radio receivers that existed back in the day. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Over time. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Many of their buttons became viewed as extra. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> And were removed. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> And with each reduction. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> These devices eventually developed. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Into the phones in our pockets. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> The scale of the object became smaller. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> And the functions of the buttons got lost. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> In the three dot menus and multilayered folders of our phones.", "alignment": {"characters": ["I", "n", "i", "t", "i", "a", "l", "l", "y", ".", " ", "<", "b", "r", "e", "a", "k", " ", "t", "i", "m", "e", "=", "\"", "0", ".", "8", "s", "\"", " ", "/", ">", " ", "T", "h", "e", " ", "c", "a", "t", "c", "h", "p", "h", "r", "a", "s", "e", " ", "l", "e", "s", "s", " ", "i", "s", " ", "m", "o", "r", "e", ".", " ", "<", "b", "r", "e", "a", "k", " ", "t", "i", "m", "e", "=", "\"", "0", ".", "8", "s", "\"", " ", "/", ">", " ", "H", "a", "d", " ", "a", " ", "s", "i", "m", "p", "l", "e", " ", "m", "e", "a", "n", "i", "n", "g", ".", " ", "<", "b", "r", "e", "a", "k", " ", "t"